is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
Routing on router0 is not participating in the campus RIP domain. From the CS Lab host, pings to Internet Lab and Server Room fail with “Destination Net Unreachable” sourced by 192.168.2.1 (router0), and the same from IT hosts via 192.168.1.1. On router0 the IPv4 routing table contains only connected networks 192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.2.0/24, and 10.0.0.0/8; there are no routes to 1.0.0.0/8 or 128.168.0.0/16, so it legitimately returns ICMP unreachables. FRR on router0 has no dynamic routing enabled: systemctl reports frr inactive (dead), /etc/frr/daemons has ripd=no, and the running config is empty; only zebra/mgmtd/staticd are present, confirming ripd is not running. Meanwhile, router1’s RIP table shows routes to 1.0.0.0/8 and 128.168.0.0/16 via router2, and it (recently) had routes for 192.168.1.0/24 and 192.168.2.0/24 via 10.10.0.1, indicating the rest of the network is healthy and exchanging routes. This isolates the CS and IT VLANs behind router0.

Root cause: RIP daemon is disabled/not running on router0, so it neither learns remote subnets nor advertises its own.

Corrective fix: Enable and start RIP on router0. Set ripd=yes in /etc/frr/daemons, create /etc/frr/ripd.conf with network 10.0.0.0, 192.168.1.0/24, and 192.168.2.0/24 (RIPv2, no auto-summary), then systemctl enable --now frr. Alternatively, add equivalent static routes pointing to 10.10.0.2. Verify routes appear and CS/IT can reach 1.0.0.0/8 and 128.168.0.0/16. 
=== END ===